Found during k48. When unreagent.exe is started from Explorer (double-click, or a file dropped onto it), Windows opens a console that closes as soon as the launcher exits, so every startup error (config error, failed MCP bind, the k48 usage error for a dropped file) is gone before the user can read it. Likely unverified until a Windows run.

Done when: if the launcher is the only process attached to its console (GetConsoleProcessList returns 1), an error exit waits for a key press (with a short hint) before closing; a launch from an existing terminal is unchanged. Win32 via syscall.NewLazyDLL, no cgo; Windows behaviour needs a real run (labelled unverified until then).

Research input 2026-11-21: docs/research/2026-11-21-windows-processes.md section 5 (console ownership, GetConsoleProcessList). Unchecked. Experiment W6 is the Windows run.

Implemented (worker): cmd/launcher/exitpause.go (pauseBeforeExit(sole, in, out): hint + read stdin byte-wise up to the first newline or a read error; no-op when not sole), exitpause_windows.go (soleConsoleClient: GetConsoleMode on stdin AND stderr must succeed, then GetConsoleProcessList with a 1-element buffer must return 1 and hold our own pid; n>1 = buffer too small = others attached, 0 = no console), exitpause_other.go (false), exitpause_test.go; main() calls the pause before both os.Exit (2 usage, 1 error). Hint: 'unreagent stopped with the error above. Press Enter to close this window.' Files named exitpause* not console*: 'console' already names the launcher command console (lifecycle.go, console_test.go). Exit paths: main os.Exit(2)/(1) covered (all run() and sub-command errors, incl. dropped file, config error, MCP bind, editor give-up exit 1, agent exit 1); success exits never wait; NOT covered: an unknown launcher flag/missing flag value (flag.CommandLine is ExitOnError, flag pkg calls os.Exit(2) itself; only reachable from a shortcut, not a double-click/drop), -h (exit 0), runtime panics. Children: run() returns only after wg.Wait, so editor and agent have ended and their jobs are closed before the pause. Not session-bound: one-off commands (RunCommand: console 'c', MCP run_command) and runtime prep (RunOnce) use context.Background; their jobs close only at process exit. A console-subsystem one still running is attached to the console -> count > 1 -> no pause, exits as before. A GUI-subsystem one-off would keep running while the prompt waits (theory, no known case). Known edge: after a session that ran the launcher console loop (editor give-up exit 1, no agent, MCP off), commandLoop's goroutine may still block on stdin and take part of the first Enter -> a second Enter may be needed (or close the window). Risk for the maintainer: any parent that gives the launcher a NEW console (start unreagent.exe, Task Scheduler with a visible console) counts as sole a