Win32 OK: GetConsoleProcessList(LPDWORD, DWORD) -> DWORD, [1]uint32 buffer, count 1; 0 = failure, >1 = required count with buffer untouched, so n==1 && ids[0]==pid is right (the pid compare is redundant but harmless). Only r1 of Call() is used, no separate GetLastError (22f8ca6 hazard not reintroduced). syscall.GetConsoleMode on os.Stdin/os.Stderr Fd works for both input and output handles; a nil os.Stdin (no handle) gives Fd()=invalid -> false. Build tags match job_windows.go. main.go:47-56: error printed before the hint, run() has returned (wg.Wait, jobs closed) before the wait, exit code 2/1 kept.
Unattended contexts: pipes/redirects (Claude Code Bash, CI runners, services) fail GetConsoleMode -> no wait. cmd/PowerShell/Windows Terminal: the shell is attached -> count >= 2 (OpenConsole/conhost is the host, not a client) -> no wait.
Findings:
1. (low, maintainer decision) exitpause_windows.go:21-25 - besides "start unreagent.exe", a Task Scheduler task set to run whether the user is logged on or not gets a hidden console with console stdin: count 1, so an error exit waits for an Enter nobody can press until the task time limit (default 72h). Same for any CREATE_NEW_CONSOLE parent. Unattended scheduled use of the launcher seems unlikely; if it matters, add a guard (e.g. IsWindowVisible(GetConsoleWindow())) or a timeout. Worth a line in the comment and the W6 run.
2. (low, known) Command-loop edge is real but safe: os.Stdin reads on Windows go through poll.FD.readLock and one shared readConsole buffer, so commandLoop goroutine and the pause read one at a time and split bytes - no panic, no data race. If the loop takes the Enter, ctx is already cancelled (NotifyContext stop deferred in run), it returns, and a second Enter is needed. Acceptable as documented.
3. (low, tests) exitpause_test.go:35 asserts a property of the constant, not of the code - harmless. exitpause_test.go:67 - a pause that ignores the read error hangs until the go test timeout instead of failing fast; a select with time.After would fail it clearly. Not blocking. The other assertions do fail when the logic breaks (reads when not sole, no hint, reads past the first line or not at all).
4. (info) Uncovered paths: flag.CommandLine ExitOnError (bad flag, exit 2) and panics. Not reachable by double-click (no args) or drop (Explorer passes full paths, never "-..."); the drop lands in unknownSubcommand -> usageError -> covered. No other os.Exit/log.Fatal in cmd/launcher.
No German, scope within cmd/ (unreagent-worker per OWNERS).

No visibility guard for a hidden console (e.g. a scheduled task): on Windows 11 with Windows Terminal as the default host a double-click runs under ConPTY, where the console window handle is a hidden pseudo-window, so an IsWindowVisible guard would likely switch the pause off in the main case. Left as is and documented in the README; revisit only if an unattended start is actually hit. Windows run (W6) still open: the manual steps are in the worker's note.

